# .claude/manifest-rules
#
# Which files this template owns, which it hands over, and which belong to the
# template project rather than to a vault. `.claude/scripts/vault-update.sh
# --generate` reads this file and writes `.claude/template-manifest` from it.
#
# One rule per line, `class<TAB>pattern`. Blank lines and lines starting with a
# hash are ignored. Patterns are ordinary shell `case` patterns matched against
# a repository-relative path, so `*` crosses a slash and `docs/*` reaches
# `docs/harnesses/aider.md` with no recursion syntax. A pattern holding `**` is
# refused, because a reader arriving from `.gitignore` would expect it to mean
# something different here and it would not.
#
# THE FIRST MATCHING RULE WINS, so the specific rules come first. That is the
# opposite direction from `.gitattributes`, where the last match wins and a
# trailing catch-all silently overrides everything above it, which this
# repository has already been bitten by. The direction here is the one a `case`
# statement already has, which is what the matcher is. A path's class is
# whatever the first matching rule below says, and the generated manifest
# records the class each path got, so read it back after generating to confirm
# a new rule landed where you meant it to.
#
# THERE IS NO CATCH-ALL, on purpose. A file matching no rule fails generation
# and fails CI, naming the path. That is the whole honesty mechanism: nobody can
# add a file to this repository without deciding what happens to it in somebody
# else's vault. Everything else here is delivery.
#
# The classes:
#
#   owned     Template machinery. The template maintains it and expects to keep
#             maintaining it. It is listed in the manifest and reported when it
#             moves.
#   seed      Shipped once, then yours. Scaffolds, examples, Obsidian config.
#             Listed in the manifest and reported, but the template does not
#             expect to maintain your copy, and its drift is summarised rather
#             than listed line by line.
#   excluded  Belongs to the template project, not to a vault. Named here so
#             the decision is recorded, and left out of the manifest entirely so
#             that no code path can reach it.
#
# Anything not tracked by git is absent from all three. That is somebody's
# notes, and the absence is what makes them unreachable.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- excluded --

# A vault does not want the template's own continuous integration. This matters
# beyond tidiness: had these been owned, a later version of the updater would
# write a workflow file into a user's GitHub repository, where it runs
# unattended, on GitHub's runners, with that repository's secrets.
excluded	.github/workflows/*
excluded	.github/ISSUE_TEMPLATE/*

# The release check the workflow above runs. It asks whether this project has
# published a release for what it ships, which is a question only this project
# has, so it sits beside the workflow rather than under `.claude/scripts/`,
# where every file is owned and would be shipped into vaults that have no
# releases to cut.
excluded	.github/release-check.sh

# Governance for the template project rather than for a vault.
excluded	CONTRIBUTING.md

# This file and the manifest it produces. The manifest cannot carry its own
# hash, and these rules are a maintainer's artefact.
excluded	.claude/manifest-rules
excluded	.claude/template-manifest

# ------------------------------------------------------------------- owned --

# The per-tier note templates come before the tier rules below, because they are
# machinery wearing a note's clothes. They describe the same frontmatter
# contract as `.claude/rules/vault-notes.md`, which is owned, so classing them
# as yours would mean a release that adds a required key ships new rules and
# stale templates. `vault-check.sh` prunes `templates/` from its scan, so
# nothing would catch the drift. The seed failure mode there is silent and the
# owned failure mode is loud.
owned	10-daily/templates/*
owned	20-projects/_logs/templates/*
owned	31-standards/templates/*
owned	40-llm-wiki/wiki/templates/*

# The structural dashboards are behaviour, not somebody's map. ARCH-INDEX.md and
# PROJECT-INDEX.md stay yours, below, because those hold what you know and which
# projects you have wired up.
owned	30-knowledge/moc/VAULT-INDEX.md

# The harness hook config, which is a hook a vault does want, unlike the two
# `.github` folders excluded above.
owned	.github/hooks/*

owned	.agents/*
owned	.claude/adapters/*
owned	.claude/agents/*
owned	.claude/githooks/*
owned	.claude/hooks/*
owned	.claude/rules/*
owned	.claude/scripts/*
owned	.claude/settings.json
owned	.claude/skills/*
owned	.codex/*
owned	.cursor/*
owned	.gemini/*
owned	.windsurf/*
owned	docs/*

owned	AGENTS.md
owned	CHANGELOG.md
owned	CLAUDE.md
owned	LICENSE
owned	VERSION
owned	.aider.conf.yml
owned	.cursorignore
owned	.geminiignore
owned	.gitattributes
owned	.gitignore
owned	opencode.json

# -------------------------------------------------------------------- seed --

# Your vault's front page. You will rewrite it, and the template's copy is of no
# use to you after the first day.
seed	README.md

# Obsidian rewrites app.json, appearance.json and graph.json whenever the
# interface changes, so these drift on every vault. That is why seed drift is
# reported as a count rather than a list.
seed	.obsidian/*

# Everything else under a content tier: the example notes the docs tell you to
# delete, the scaffolds you are meant to rewrite, and the .gitkeep files that
# hold the empty folders open.
seed	01-inbox/*
seed	10-daily/*
seed	20-projects/*
seed	30-knowledge/*
seed	31-standards/*
seed	40-llm-wiki/*
seed	90-auto-memory/*
seed	99-archive/*
